    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d743">

                <pb facs="0839" n="829" sameAs="frus:frus1943v01/pg829"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">550.AD1/67: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Argentina</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Armour</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Buenos Aires</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-04-08T18:00:00-03:00">April 8, 1943—6
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received April 9—1:24 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>752. My telegram No. 711, April 2, 6 p.m.<anchor n="19" xml:id="d743-f1"/>
                    Exclusion of Argentina from invitation to post-war food and monetary
                        conferences<anchor n="20" xml:id="d743-f2"/>
                    <pb facs="0840" n="830" xml:id="pg830"/> has caused a profound impression here,
                    although owing state of siege regulations press comment has, with few exceptions
                    which are being reported by airmail, been extremely guarded.</p>
                <p>One of my colleagues informs me that the Finance Minister<anchor n="21"
                        xml:id="d743-f3"/> professed pained surprise that Argentina, one of the
                    world’s greatest grain and meat producing countries, should not have been
                    invited to the food conference.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Armour</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
